general_notes.general_notes:
This is an important early application of normative modeling to characterize individual-level heterogeneity in ADHD using voxel-based morphometry. The study was conducted at a single site (Radboud University Medical Center) using a single 1.5T scanner, avoiding multi-site complications. All participants were Dutch and of European Caucasian ancestry. The ADHD diagnosis was based on structured Diagnostic Interview for ADHD in Adults (DIVA), confirming both childhood history and current persistent ADHD. Inclusion criteria included: DSM-IV-TR criteria met in childhood and adulthood, no psychosis, no substance use disorder, IQ>70, no neurological disorders, no sensorimotor disabilities, no medication except psychostimulants/atomoxetine. Healthy controls had no neurological/mental disorders and no first-degree relatives with ADHD or major mental disorders. About 11.8% of ADHD participants were currently on stimulant medication, 76.4% had past medication use, and 11.8% had no medication history. The normative model characterized typical brain changes: gray matter showed global decreases from age 20-70 years with largest decreases in frontal and cerebellar regions; white matter showed both increases (parietal, temporal) and decreases (frontal, thalamic) with age. Sex-specific modeling was performed due to sex effects in ADHD. The study is conceptually aligned with Research Domain Criteria (RDoC) initiative emphasizing multi-level, high-dimensional descriptions of individual patients rather than simple group comparisons.


